{"result": {"response": {"id": "chatcmpl-ETWNnMv72qjNOWKUfEPNZF58aeqy7", "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "logprobs": null, "message": {"content": "**Transition temperature** is a measured mechanical and toughness property associated with the ductile-to-brittle fracture transition. It is strongly influenced by steel processing, microstructure, grain size, precipitation, dislocation hardening, deformation, recovery, recrystallization, reduction, finishing and deformation temperatures, and the formation of separations.\n\nThe transition temperature generally increases when strengthening mechanisms such as vanadium precipitation hardening, coherent precipitates in the ferrite matrix, and dislocation hardening become more pronounced. It reflects the balance between these hardening effects and the toughness-improving effects of grain refinement, recovery, recrystallization, and favorable microstructural refinement. Its relationship with grain size has been described by Heslop and Petch.\n\nTransition temperature decreases as the number of separations increases and also decreases with increasing deformation in the gamma–alpha two-phase region. It decreases linearly with increasing total reduction in processing ranges 2 and 3. During continuum rolling, lowering the finishing temperature within the alpha region reduces the transition temperature; consequently, controlled-rolled steel generally has a lower transition temperature than conventionally hot-rolled, normalized, or quenched-and-tempered steel. The property is usually substantially higher in hot-rolled strip than in plate.\n\nMicrostructure has a major effect. Acicular ferrite exhibits a lower transition temperature than bainitic structures because its smaller effective facet units improve resistance to brittle fracture. Precipitation hardening, separations, and the specific ferritic or bainitic structure can nevertheless raise or lower the measured value depending on their competing effects.\n\nThe transition temperature is also anisotropic. It is typically highest in the diagonal, or 45-degree, direction and lower in the longitudinal and transverse directions. Differences exceeding 100 K may occur between the longitudinal and through-thickness (L and Z) directions. In the Z direction, separations disappear when the temperature reaches the transition temperature.", "refusal": null, "role": "assistant", "annotations": null, "audio": null, "function_call": null, "tool_calls": null, "provider_specific_fields": {"refusal": null}}, "provider_specific_fields": {}}], "created": 1790704699, "model": "gpt-5.6-luna", "object": "chat.completion", "moderation": null, "service_tier": "default", "system_fingerprint": null, "usage": {"completion_tokens": 488, "prompt_tokens": 547, "total_tokens": 1035, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": 0, "audio_tokens": 0, "reasoning_tokens": 96, "rejected_prediction_tokens": 0}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": 0, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "cached_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_tokens": 0}}, "formatted_response": null, "obfuscation": "Ji6GEPwN1APBqb", "content": "**Transition temperature** is a measured mechanical and toughness property associated with the ductile-to-brittle fracture transition. It is strongly influenced by steel processing, microstructure, grain size, precipitation, dislocation hardening, deformation, recovery, recrystallization, reduction, finishing and deformation temperatures, and the formation of separations.\n\nThe transition temperature generally increases when strengthening mechanisms such as vanadium precipitation hardening, coherent precipitates in the ferrite matrix, and dislocation hardening become more pronounced. It reflects the balance between these hardening effects and the toughness-improving effects of grain refinement, recovery, recrystallization, and favorable microstructural refinement. Its relationship with grain size has been described by Heslop and Petch.\n\nTransition temperature decreases as the number of separations increases and also decreases with increasing deformation in the gamma–alpha two-phase region. It decreases linearly with increasing total reduction in processing ranges 2 and 3. During continuum rolling, lowering the finishing temperature within the alpha region reduces the transition temperature; consequently, controlled-rolled steel generally has a lower transition temperature than conventionally hot-rolled, normalized, or quenched-and-tempered steel. The property is usually substantially higher in hot-rolled strip than in plate.\n\nMicrostructure has a major effect. Acicular ferrite exhibits a lower transition temperature than bainitic structures because its smaller effective facet units improve resistance to brittle fracture. Precipitation hardening, separations, and the specific ferritic or bainitic structure can nevertheless raise or lower the measured value depending on their competing effects.\n\nThe transition temperature is also anisotropic. It is typically highest in the diagonal, or 45-degree, direction and lower in the longitudinal and transverse directions. Differences exceeding 100 K may occur between the longitudinal and through-thickness (L and Z) directions. In the Z direction, separations disappear when the temperature reaches the transition temperature."}, "metrics": {"attempted_request_count": 1, "successful_response_count": 0, "failed_response_count": 0, "responses_with_tokens": 1, "prompt_tokens": 547, "completion_tokens": 488, "total_tokens": 1035, "compute_duration_seconds": 7.898813486099243, "streaming_responses": 0, "retries": 0, "requests_with_retries": 0}}}